<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-overdamped-analysis" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Analyzing Overdamped and Critically Damped Motion</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SUMMARY ASSEMBLAGE: OVERDAMPED + CRITICALLY DAMPED
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Overdamped and critically damped systems share the same qualitative behavior:
    the mass returns to equilibrium without oscillating, passing through it at most once.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-overdamped-critical-summary">
    <title>Summary: Overdamped and Critically Damped Cases of <m>mx'' + cx' + kx = 0</m></title>
    <tabular halign="left" row-headers="yes">
      <col width="22%"/>
      <col width="36%"/>
      <col width="20%"/>
      <col width="22%"/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell>Case</cell>
        <cell>Discriminant and roots</cell>
        <cell>Crossings of <m>x=0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty} x(t)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Overdamped</term> (larger <m>c</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>c^2 > 4km</m> (two distinct real roots)</cell>
        <cell>At most once</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Critically damped</term> (borderline)</cell>
        <cell><m>c^2 = 4km</m> (one repeated real root)</cell>
        <cell>At most once</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SETUP: INTRODUCE THE EXAMPLE FUNCTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    For the rest of this page we analyze the position function
    <me>x(t) = 4e^{-2t} - 10e^{-t}.</me>
    We will use information about this function to build a complete picture
    of the motion.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXPLORATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exploration xml:id="exploration-overdamped-analysis">
    <title>Building a Picture of the Motion</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Throughout this exploration, <m>x(t) = 4e^{-2t} - 10e^{-t}</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Task 1: Which damping case -->

    <task xml:id="task-overdamped-which-case">
      <title>Which Damping Case?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Based on the form of <m>x(t) = 4e^{-2t} - 10e^{-t}</m>,
          which damping case are we in?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Overdamped</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! The solution is a sum of two distinct decaying exponentials,
              <m>x(t) = c_1 e^{r_1 t} + c_2 e^{r_2 t}</m> with <m>r_1 = -2</m>
              and <m>r_2 = -1</m>. Two distinct real roots means <m>c^2 > 4km</m>,
              which is the overdamped case. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Critically damped</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. A critically damped solution has the form
              <m>x(t) = c_1 e^{-at} + c_2 t e^{-at}</m>, with one exponential
              and one multiplied by <m>t</m>. Here we have two different
              exponential rates, so the roots are distinct, not repeated.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Underdamped</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. An underdamped solution has the form
              <m>x(t) = e^{-pt}(c_1\cos(\omega t) + c_2\sin(\omega t))</m>,
              which involves oscillating trig terms. This solution has no
              trig functions, so it decays without oscillating.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Task 2: Equilibrium crossing -->

    <task xml:id="task-overdamped-equilibrium">
      <title>Does the Mass Pass Through Equilibrium?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Will the mass ever pass through the equilibrium position <m>x = 0</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Set <m>x(t) = 0</m> and solve for <m>t</m>. Remember that
          only <m>t \geq 0</m> is physically meaningful.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <details>
          <summary>Full solution</summary>
          <p>
            We solve <m>4e^{-2t} - 10e^{-t} = 0</m>.
            Since <m>e^{-t} \neq 0</m>, divide both sides by <m>e^{-t}</m>:
            <me>4e^{-t} - 10 = 0 \implies e^{-t} = \frac{10}{4} = \frac{5}{2}
               \implies t = -\ln\!\left(\tfrac{5}{2}\right) \approx -0.92.</me>
            Since <m>t \lt 0</m>, this is not a valid time. The mass
            <alert>never passes through equilibrium</alert> for <m>t \geq 0</m>.
          </p>
        </details>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Task 3: Initial conditions -->

    <task xml:id="task-overdamped-initial">
      <title>Initial Position and Velocity</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m>. What do these tell you about
          where the mass starts and which direction it initially moves?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Recall: <m>x(0) > 0</m> means stretched, <m>x(0) \lt 0</m> means compressed.
          <m>x'(0) > 0</m> means moving in the positive direction (toward equilibrium
          when compressed), <m>x'(0) \lt 0</m> means moving in the negative direction.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <details>
          <summary>Work</summary>
          <p>
            <alert>Initial position:</alert>
            <me>x(0) = 4 - 10 = -6.</me>
            Since <m>x(0) = -6 \lt 0</m>, the mass starts <alert>compressed</alert>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Initial velocity:</alert> Differentiating,
            <me>x'(t) = -8e^{-2t} + 10e^{-t},</me>
            so <m>x'(0) = -8 + 10 = 2 > 0</m>.
            The mass is initially moving in the <alert>positive direction</alert>,
            that is, toward the equilibrium position.
          </p>
          <p>
            Together: the mass starts compressed at <m>x = -6</m>, moves
            toward equilibrium, but (from Task 2) never reaches it.
            So the mass stays compressed for all <m>t \geq 0</m>.
          </p>
        </details>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Task 4: Matching -->

    <task xml:id="task-overdamped-matching">
      <title>Sketch the Graph</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          From Task 3 we observed that <m>x(0) = -6</m> and <m>x'(0) = 2 > 0</m>, so:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>x(0) \lt 0</m>: the mass starts <alert>compressed</alert>, and</p></li>
            <li><p><m>x'(0) = 2 > 0</m>: it is initially moving in the <alert>positive direction</alert>.</p></li>
          </ul>
          Using this, together with the fact that the mass never crosses equilibrium
          (Task 2), determine which graph below matches <m>x(t) = 4e^{-2t} - 10e^{-t}</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-overdamped-matching">
          <caption>
            Three candidate graphs for <m>x(t) = 4e^{-2t} - 10e^{-t}</m>.
            The dashed green line marks the equilibrium <m>x = 0</m>.
            The red dot marks the starting position at <m>t = 0</m>.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="interactive-overdamped-matching"
            iframe="ch3-graph/static-overdamped-matching.html"
            width="100%" aspect="5:2">
            <description>
              Three side-by-side candidate graphs labeled (a), (b), and (c),
              each with a dashed green equilibrium line at x equals zero and
              a red dot marking the starting position at t equals zero.
              Graph (a): starts below zero (compressed), initially moves further
              below zero becoming more compressed, then turns around and increases
              back toward zero from below, never crossing the equilibrium.
              Graph (b): starts below zero (compressed), immediately moves toward
              zero in the positive direction, approaching the equilibrium from
              below without ever crossing it.
              Graph (c): starts below zero and oscillates above and below the
              equilibrium line multiple times with decreasing amplitude,
              eventually approaching zero.
            </description>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Graph (a)</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Graph (a) does start compressed, which is correct. However, it then
              moves further from equilibrium before turning around, meaning
              <m>x'(0) \lt 0</m> initially. But we found <m>x'(0) = 2 > 0</m>,
              so the mass is moving toward equilibrium from the very start,
              not away from it.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Graph (b)</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Graph (b) starts below zero (compressed), moves in the
              positive direction toward equilibrium, and never crosses <m>x = 0</m>,
              exactly matching what we found in Tasks 2 and 3. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Graph (c)</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Graph (c) shows oscillations, which is the behavior of an underdamped
              system. But <m>x(t) = 4e^{-2t} - 10e^{-t}</m> has two distinct real
              exponential terms with no trig functions, so it is overdamped and
              does not oscillate.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

  </exploration>
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       TRANSITION TO EXAMPLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    The key idea here was using the following information:
    <ol>
      <li><p>the sign of <m>x(0)</m> (does it start compressed, stretched, or at equilibrium?),</p></li>
      <li><p>the sign of <m>x'(0)</m> (is it initially moving left or right?), and</p></li>
      <li><p>whether the mass ever crosses the equilibrium position.</p></li>
    </ol>
    These three pieces of information will always allow you to give a sketch of the graph,
    as we did in part (c).
  </p>

  <p>
    Now that we have a clear picture of the motion, let's use what we found to
    answer some more specific questions about the spring's behavior.
  </p>
  <example xml:id="ex-overdamped-analysis">
    <title>Analyzing <m>x(t) = 4e^{-2t} - 10e^{-t}</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose the position function of a mass in a spring-mass-dashpot system is
        <me>x(t) = 4e^{-2t} - 10e^{-t}, \quad t \geq 0.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Will the mass pass through the equilibrium position?</p></li>
          <li><p>When is the spring most stretched, if at all?</p></li>
          <li><p>When is the spring most compressed, if at all?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <task xml:id="ex-task-overdamped-equil">
      <title>Will the mass pass through equilibrium?</title>
      <hint>
        <p>
          This was solved in <xref ref="task-overdamped-equilibrium"/> of the exploration.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          From <xref ref="task-overdamped-equilibrium"/>: the only solution to
          <m>x(t) = 0</m> occurs at <m>t = -\ln(5/2) \approx -0.92 \lt 0</m>,
          which is not a valid time. The mass <alert>never passes through equilibrium</alert>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-task-overdamped-stretched">
      <title>When is the spring most stretched?</title>
      <hint>
        <p>
          What can you deduce from the sketch of the graph in the exploration?
          Think about what the initial conditions and the equilibrium crossing
          result together tell you about whether the spring is ever stretched.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          From the exploration: the mass starts compressed (<m>x(0) = -6 \lt 0</m>)
          and never crosses the equilibrium position. Therefore <m>x(t) \lt 0</m>
          for all <m>t \geq 0</m>, so the spring is <alert>never stretched</alert>.
          This is confirmed by the graph below, which stays entirely below <m>x = 0</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-overdamped-stretched">
          <caption>
            The graph of <m>x(t) = 4e^{-2t} - 10e^{-t}</m> stays below zero for
            all <m>t \geq 0</m>. The spring is never stretched.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="interactive-overdamped-stretched"
            iframe="ch3-graph/static-overdamped-example.html"
            width="100%" aspect="5:2">
            <description>
              Graph of x of t equals 4 e to the negative 2t minus 10 e to the
              negative t, for t from 0 to 6. The curve starts at x equals
              negative 6, increases toward the equilibrium dashed line at x equals
              zero, and approaches zero from below without ever crossing it.
            </description>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-task-overdamped-compressed">
      <title>When is the spring most compressed?</title>
      <hint>
        <p>
          What can you deduce from the sketch of the graph that used knowledge
          of <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m>? Think about the direction of motion
          from the very start.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          From the exploration: <m>x(0) = -6</m> and <m>x'(0) = 2 > 0</m>,
          so the mass starts compressed and is already moving toward equilibrium
          at <m>t = 0</m>. Since it never crosses equilibrium, it moves closer
          to zero for all <m>t > 0</m>. Therefore <m>x(0) = -6</m> is the
          most negative value, and the spring is <alert>most compressed at <m>t = 0</m></alert>.
          The graph confirms this: the curve starts at its lowest point and
          increases from there.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-overdamped-compressed">
          <caption>
            The spring is most compressed at <m>t = 0</m>, where <m>x(0) = -6</m>
            is the minimum. The mass moves toward equilibrium for all <m>t > 0</m>.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="interactive-overdamped-compressed"
            iframe="ch3-graph/static-overdamped-example.html"
            width="100%" aspect="5:2">
            <description>
              Same graph of x of t equals 4 e to the negative 2t minus 10 e to
              the negative t. The red dot at t equals 0, x equals negative 6
              marks the most compressed position. The curve increases from
              there toward zero.
            </description>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      To sketch the graph of <m>x(t)</m> and answer questions about the motion,
      we should begin by finding:
      <ol>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The sign of <m>x(0)</m>:</alert> does the mass start compressed,
          stretched, or at equilibrium?
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The sign of <m>x'(0)</m>:</alert> which direction does the mass
          initially move?
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Whether the mass ever crosses equilibrium:</alert> for overdamped
          and critically damped systems, this happens at most once.
        </p></li>
      </ol>
      These three pieces of information are enough to sketch the graph of
      <m>x(t)</m>, and from that sketch we can answer qualitative questions
      about the behavior of the system.
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="video-overdamped-analysis">
    <caption>This video goes through the content of this page.</caption>
    <video youtube="4zwhMFCQJyc" width="100%" preview="default"/>
  </figure>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-4-2"
    iframe="question-form.html?section=3.4.2"
    width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>

</section>
